Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, appealing for international aid as Cyclone Ditwah’s torrential rains and floods resulted in 132 fatalities and left 176 individuals unaccounted for. Over 15,000 residences were demolished and 78,000 individuals sought refuge in government-run temporary shelters, according to the Disaster Management Centre (DMC), as reported by AFP. DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda confirmed, “We have 132 confirmed dead and another 176 missing,” and noted that relief efforts have been bolstered with forces from the army, navy, and air force. As the cyclone moved north toward India, the government urged international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to donate funds to support nearly half a million people impacted by the calamity. India promptly responded, dispatching two aircraft loaded with relief items, and an Indian warship docked in Colombo contributed its provisions. Prime Minister Narendra Modi, via a post on X, conveyed his condolences to Sri Lanka, mourning the cyclone's victims and reaffirming India’s unwavering support for its "closest maritime neighbour,” emphasizing that India swiftly provided relief supplies and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment. Despite the cyclone’s northward trajectory, new landslides occurred in Kandy, 115 km (70 miles) east of Colombo, inundating sections of the primary access road, and worsening floods in low-lying regions led authorities to mandate evacuations for residents along the Kelani River.
