data.fused_content:
The nation might still face its 16th storm of the year as 2025 draws to a close, according to Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien. Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department, presented an overview of the year's natural disaster and hydro-meteorological conditions, highlighting that 2025 has experienced exceptionally atypical weather and disaster patterns, resulting in significant damage across the country. Notably, the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue recorded an extraordinary 1,739 mm of rainfall within a single day in October, a figure the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is in the process of assessing to potentially acknowledge it as one of the world’s highest recorded 24-hour rainfall totals.

The East Sea experienced an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression occurrences, marking the highest activity level recorded since Vietnam's documentation began in 1961. By the close of November, a total of 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented, exceeding the former high of 20 established in 2017. Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) achieved level 17 wind speeds, with gusts surpassing this level, marking it as the most powerful storm ever documented in the East Sea. Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) emerged in the East Sea in June, an occurrence not witnessed in over four decades. During late November, a tropical depression transitioned from the Indian Ocean to the northwestern Pacific, marking an unprecedented path in the annals of meteorological records.

The floods of 2025 were notably extensive and intense, with river levels reaching unprecedented heights across numerous areas, stretching from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands, further impacting the lower Mekong Delta. Many rivers experienced peak water levels that exceeded past historical highs. For the first time in a single year, 20 rivers recorded either extreme or historic flooding. Significantly, the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, which was once a section of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin, encompassing the Cau River (Thai Nguyen, Bac Ninh), the Thuong River (Bac Giang), the Bang Giang River (Cao Bang), and the Lo River (Tuyen Quang.

The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding directly impacted by climate change.
