The Labor government plans to allocate $200 million to fund 96 projects nationwide, aimed at bolstering community resilience against severe weather conditions. This includes initiatives on Palm Island, located off the coast of Queensland, which faces increased threats from tropical cyclones and intense storms. Here, an 800-person capacity cyclone shelter and evacuation center are planned for construction. The $200 million federal investment will see over half allocated to infrastructure projects with immediate construction initiation, including establishing recovery centers, flood protection infrastructure, reducing the dangers posed by bushfires, and implementing sophisticated alert systems for natural threats. Additional initiatives feature a $1.6 million infrastructure undertaking aimed at safeguarding Townsvale Estate in South Australia, a $14.1 million investment for enhancing stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, located in Central NSW, and $895k to enhance the capacity of Karajarri Rangers, focusing on reducing severe wildfire threats in northwest WA.

This declaration marks the third round of financial support from the $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a governmental program dedicated to enhancing disaster resilience and mitigating risks. In the past two rounds, the DRF has distributed approximately $400 million in federal funding to over 350 projects. Almost 50% of these projects are currently in the pre-construction or construction-delivery stages, with 43 projects already approaching completion or finalized.

"Australians are well-acquainted with the annual onslaught of floods, bushfires, and cyclones, which is precisely why the Albanese government initiated the Disaster Ready Fund, allocating up to $1 billion over the span of five years," stated Kristy McBain, the emergency management minister. The third round will advantage every one of the 538 local government areas spanning all states and territories. Crucially, the investment elevates to $600 million – and
