A remarkably intense storm struck in November, churning over the South China Sea as it neared Vietnam. Its winds were consistently blowing at speeds of approximately 114 mph, with gusts potentially climbing to 137 mph. Kalmaegi is predicted to deliver over 24 inches of rain in certain regions.

The central provinces of Vietnam are currently experiencing severe flooding due to record-breaking rains, with Kalmaegi expected to unleash over 24 inches of rain in certain regions. In coastal cities like Danang, waves up to 10ft high battered the coast, and numerous residences in Quy Nhon also experienced prolonged power outages. Officials cautioned that as much as 4 inches of rain might flood regions at lower elevations. High tides were anticipated on the Saigon River.

In the Philippines, Kalmaegi claimed the lives of at least 114 individuals and left 127 unaccounted for, prompting President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. to announce a state of emergency on Thursday. Having unleashed devastation across the Philippines, with the central province of Cebu bearing the brunt, the tropical cyclone exited the archipelago on Wednesday, heading into the South China Sea.

In the town of Liloan, Cebu, Krizza Espra visited the mortuary, where the remains of her husband and three children, victims of a roof collapse, were prepared for the upcoming wake. She also explained that four members of her family – her mother and aunt among them – are still unaccounted for.

The typhoon forced the displacement of over 560,000 villagers in the Philippines, with close to 450,000 being relocated to emergency shelters, according to the Office of Civil Defence. With Mr. Marcos's declaration of a "state of national calamity," the government is enabled to expedite the release of emergency funds and curb both food hoarding and excessive pricing.

Six fatalities linked to Kalmaegi occurred when a Philippine military helicopter met a tragic end in the southern province of Agusan del Sur on Tuesday; the crew was en route to deliver humanitarian aid to areas heavily impacted by the typhoon. Officials in charge of disaster response have cautioned that an approaching tropical cyclone from the Pacific may intensify into a super typhoon, potentially striking the northern Philippines at the start of next week.

Kalmaegi unleashed a deluge equivalent to one-and-a-half months of precipitation in merely one day on Tuesday across metropolitan Cebu, according to state forecaster Benison Estareja. It triggered flash floods and led to the swelling of a river and other waterways in Cebu city as well as in nearby towns. Torrential floodwaters engulfed or carried off numerous vehicles in Cebu's residential areas, with dramatic footage captured by residents who were marooned on rooftops. A minimum of 71 fatalities occurred in Cebu, primarily attributed to drowning incidents, and authorities also noted that an additional 62 individuals were unaccounted for in the central province of Negros Occidental, close to Cebu. Sixty-five individuals remained unaccounted for and 69 suffered injuries.

“We took all possible measures for the typhoon, but, you know, unforeseen events like flash floods can still occur,” Governor Pamela Baricuatro conveyed to The Associated Press over the phone. Ms. Baricuatro stated that the storm's impacts might have been aggravated by quarrying activities that led to river blockage and overflow, combined with inadequate flood control measures in Cebu province. A scandal surrounding inadequate or entirely absent flood control projects throughout the Philippines has ignited public indignation and led to street demonstrations in recent months.

Moreover, Cebu continues to grapple with the aftermath of a 6.9 magnitude earthquake that struck on September 30, which resulted in at least 79 fatalities and the displacement of numerous individuals.
