In the first nine months of 2025, extreme weather events in India were linked to approximately 4,064 fatalities, according to a joint report from the Delhi-based Centre for Science and Environment (CSE) and Down To Earth (DTE). The analysis highlighted a 48 percent rise in such deaths over the past four years.

Madhya Pradesh recorded the highest death toll, while Maharashtra suffered the most in terms of damaged agricultural land, with 8.4 million hectares affected. The impact of these climatic events — including heat and cold waves, lightning, storms, heavy rainfall, floods, and landslides — resulted in an astonishing 9.47 million hectares of crop damage, marking a 400 percent increase over four years.

Throughout this period, the nation experienced extreme weather conditions on 99 percent of the days, resulting in these recorded losses. A minimum of 18 states or Union territories experienced their peak in extreme weather days since 2022. From February through September in 2025, no fewer than 30 states and Union Territories faced persistent extreme weather conditions for eight consecutive months. Himachal Pradesh witnessed the highest number of these events.

The Climate India 2025 report, an annual publication released on November 19, 2025, during a virtual seminar hosted by Sunita Narain, draws upon close to 1,500 days of continuous observation, documenting seasonal patterns in extreme weather beginning in 2022. India's monsoon continues to be the most destructive season.

“What we need to understand is the scale — the scale of mitigation that Belém is talking about, the scale on which the whole world has to come together,” remarked Sunita Narain, the director general of CSE and editor for DTE, adding, “Given the intensity and frequency of extreme weather events, But it is also about what we need to do, keeping in mind that there will be more and more such disasters.”
