Singapore: Over 1200 lives have been claimed by severe weather in southern Asia over the past two weeks, with expectations that the toll will increase as rescuers search through mud and debris for the hundreds still unaccounted for. Late last month, simultaneous tropical storms triggered floods and mudslides that obliterated entire villages in Sri Lanka and on the Indonesian island of Sumatra.

Confronted by increasing fatalities from recent occurrences, Indonesian President Prabowo Subianto has urged for increased efforts to combat climate change, while Sri Lankan President Anura Kumara Dissanayake remarked that this is an unprecedented occurrence of such a calamity impacting the whole nation. Hat Yai, a city in southern Thailand, has been hit by what officials are calling a once-in-300-years rainfall occurrence. Hat Yai, the administrative hub of the province, experienced over 600 millimeters of rainfall within a three-day period starting November 19.

The primary factor behind the destruction in Indonesia was Cyclone Senyar, which unleashed a month's worth of rainfall, totaling 382 millimeters, within just a 24-hour span starting on November 25. Over 659 individuals have lost their lives in Aceh, North Sumatra, and West Sumatra. According to Indonesia’s Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, the rainfall intensified due to Senyar's interaction with Typhoon Koto, a separate weather system situated over the Philippines. On November 27, Senyar shifted back across the strait in a diminished state heading towards Malaysia.

In Sri Lanka, where approximately 30,000 individuals perished during the 2004 Boxing Day tsunami, Dissanayake described the current and ongoing calamity as the “largest and most challenging natural disaster” the nation has ever faced. Cyclone Ditwah lingered along the entire eastern shoreline for over two days starting late last week, resulting in the deaths of at least 390 individuals and leaving hundreds unaccounted for, as approximately 180,000 individuals have had to seek refuge in government safety centers. According to United Nations estimates, approximately 1 million individuals across all 25 districts of Sri Lanka have been impacted.

In Indonesia, nearly 1.5 million individuals, with over 500,000 forced to leave their homes, have faced impacts across 48 regencies in Sumatra, as reported by the National Disaster Management Agency.

“We must confront climate change effectively,” Prabowo stated while touring the impacted regions. “Municipal authorities are crucial in protecting the environment and readying for the severe weather scenarios that future climate change is poised to bring,” Prabowo remarked.

Local authorities are crucial in protecting the environment and gearing up for the severe weather events that future climate change will bring. Locals have expressed disapproval towards Anutin Charnvirakul's recently established national government, accusing it of failing to deliver what they believed to be adequate alerts regarding the intensity of the weather system and the imminent floods. Anutin apologized for the government’s performance, yet continued to stand firm. “There is no need to say they’re wrong because everyone has the right to speak,” he informed the local press. “However, regarding our efforts in Hat Yai – assisting individuals and executing initiatives – the government is assured that we are proceeding correctly and fulfilling all necessary actions.”
