The death toll from floods and landslides on Sumatra island in Indonesia climbed to 708 on Tuesday, according to the national disaster agency, with 504 individuals unaccounted for. This marked a significant rise from the 604 fatalities reported by the agency on Monday. Initially, the number was stated as 753, but it was later adjusted to 708 by Tuesday evening. The region has been ravaged by intense monsoon rains and a rare tropical storm formed in the Malacca strait, devastating parts of Sumatra and southern Thailand, where 181 people were killed. The season’s monsoons often bring heavy rains that can trigger landslides and floods, but this year’s downpours were compounded by the storm, impacting parts of Asia such as Indonesia, Sri Lanka, and southern Thailand, with more than 1,300 lives lost, infrastructure damaged, and towns flooded across the area. Rescuers and aid teams are urgently attempting to reach those affected, though their efforts are hindered by impassable roads and destroyed bridges. UNICEF noted that areas like Tapanuli Tengah in North Sumatra and Agam in Aceh are inaccessible by road. Aceh, severely impacted, faces shortages of essentials such as rice and vegetables, with prices having tripled, according to Islamic Relief, which is dispatching 12 tonnes of emergency food supplies. The charity warned that if supply lines aren't restored within the week, communities could face severe hunger. On Monday, the Indonesian government announced the delivery of 34,000 tonnes of rice and 6.8 million litres of cooking oil to Aceh, North Sumatra, and West Sumatra. Communities throughout Aceh face the threat of severe food shortages and hunger if supply chains are not restored within the next week. Approximately 3.2 million people have been affected, and 2,600 have suffered injuries. One million residents have been evacuated from high-risk zones. Many survivors, currently housed in evacuation centers, recounted how torrential water swept in suddenly, inundating villages. The World Health Organization is deploying rapid response teams and essential supplies to the affected area, while also enhancing disease monitoring efforts. WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus emphasized that this situation serves as a stark reminder of how climate change is leading to more frequent and intense weather events, with devastating consequences. Rains have eased across the country, but landslide alerts remain in force across most of the hardest-hit central region, officials said.
