An increased threat of severe weather conditions along Ireland's coast poses a risk to countless homes and businesses as experts brace for the worst-case scenario. Rising sea levels and more intense storms can result in heightened storm surges along coastlines, affecting over 104,000 properties. Gamma Risk, a company that provides property risk insights, has highlighted the likelihood of rising insurance expenses stemming from more powerful storms fueled by climate change, with costs potentially escalating to $5.2 billion (€4.5 billion) by the year 2050. Data from Gamma Risk reveals that 4.5% of properties are susceptible to flooding during severe coastal events. Coastal flooding in Ireland represents a tangible and growing financial burden, according to Richard Garry, the chief commercial officer at Gamma Risk. Climate Ireland reported that coastal cities like Cork, Dublin, Galway, and Limerick are at higher risk. Human activity can create heat-trapping pollution that intensifies extreme weather and puts communities in danger. Human activity has emitted about 35 billion tons of carbon dioxide each year, according to the Environmental Protection Agency. Oceans absorb around 90% of the heat from these emissions, and scientists have identified that this process, known as thermal expansion, accounts for one-third of the global sea-level rise over the past twenty years. As explained by the Natural Resources Defense Council, increasing temperatures lead to melting ice sheets and rising sea levels. Rising sea levels pose a threat to coastal communities worldwide, impacting properties and livelihoods due to increased flooding and fiercer storms. Although extreme weather has always been part of our world, researchers have underscored how human actions can amplify their intensity and danger. Addressing climate challenges begins with comprehending the underlying issues.
