Severe weather across several states has claimed at least two lives, with intense winds and towering waves sweeping two men off Frankston Pier in Melbourne. In New South Wales, record-breaking temperatures have been observed. A farmer in Victoria, Adrian Coatsworth, felt a sense of relief when rain finally reached the Mallee region, describing it as a slight 8mm respite at the end of the season right before the harvest, which will be beneficial. He noted that although it may be somewhat delayed for a few, they have opted to proceed with carrying the wheat forward. Coatsworth stated that achieving break-even or earning a modest profit would be incredibly satisfying after a difficult year that began with a dry summer, and that temperate weather conditions have been advantageous. Farmers are anticipating summer rainfall, and specialists indicate a possibility of a La Nina occurrence this spring.

Dr. Kimberley Reid, an atmospheric scientist at the University of Melbourne, explained that La Nina acts as the counterpart to El Nino, amplifying trade winds and typically increasing cloud cover and precipitation. The US Weather Agency reported the onset of La Nina conditions in the equatorial Pacific Ocean at the start of this month, while the World Meteorology Organisation indicated these conditions are likely to be weak. Australia finds itself in the midst of a tug of war, influenced by developments in the Pacific with a possible weak La Nina, the Indian Ocean with the Indian Ocean Dipole, and activities near Antarctica, creating considerable uncertainty in the spring and summer weather predictions. The Bureau of Meteorology characterizes the present Australian climate as neutral, yet forecasts suggest a potential for higher than average rainfall from November through January.

High-velocity winds resembling cyclone conditions left thousands without electricity across Victoria. Andrew Gissing, CEO of Natural Hazards Research Australia, urges all communities to remain vigilant about the risks of natural hazards and to familiarize themselves with those living around them. He also recommends ensuring you have the emergency alert apps specific to your state or territory installed, and above all, prioritizing looking out for one another. This involves preparing for a variety of natural calamities.
