The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) of the United Nations advocates for broadening global monitoring and forecasting systems to warn individuals, particularly in developing countries, about severe weather conditions. “Early warning means early action,” asserted WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo during her address at the UN's yearly conference in Geneva.

A shift in the global climate has led to more frequent and severe occurrences of extreme weather events, such as hurricanes, flooding, wildfires, and glacier collapses. The WMO has noted that over the past five decades, climate-related incidents have resulted in the deaths of more than 2 million individuals, and almost 50% of countries face heightened exposure to these weather phenomena, with developing nations being especially susceptible. The agency expresses its worry over inadequate defenses against these weather phenomena and asserts that implementing early alert systems could avert most of the fatalities resulting from such conditions.

While advancements have been achieved and more nations are adopting early warning systems, the WMO acknowledged that much remains to be accomplished. Half of the countries utilizing the systems are engaging only with their fundamental features, while 16% are operating below even these basic levels. A warning system designed for early detection grants additional time for evacuations and preparations in the event of impending extreme weather impacting a region. Early warning systems are crucial for providing individuals with the chance to bolster their surroundings or evacuate safely.

The WMO is focused on raising awareness about the underuse of early warning systems for extreme weather occurrences. With the implementation of effective systems in more nations, a greater number of lives can be preserved. The WMO emphasized that climate-related weather events are on the rise, noting that "impacts are escalating as weather conditions grow more severe." Efforts are underway to establish early warning systems, yet further efforts are necessary.
