India ranked ninth among the countries most impacted by extreme weather events from 1995 to 2024, according to the latest Climate Risk Index (CRI) 2026 report by Germanwatch. The evaluation was unveiled at the current COP30 event in Belem, Brazil, marking the second edition of the report to be issued this year. India was positioned at 15th place in the rankings for 2024.

The Climate Risk Index (CRI) analyzes rapid onset occurrences such as floods, storms, extreme temperatures, wildfires, glacial lake outbursts, and floods, excluding slow onset phenomena like increasing mean temperatures, sea level rise, ocean acidification, and glacial retreat. The higher a country's position on the list, the more severely it has been impacted by extreme weather events linked to human-induced climate change.

Over the past thirty years, India has experienced approximately 430 extreme weather events, including floods, landslides, heatwaves, cyclones, and droughts, impacting over a billion people nationwide. These events have caused inflation-adjusted damages totaling approximately $170 billion, and have claimed the lives of over 80,000 individuals. Subsequently, heat waves impacted approximately 33 million individuals, while droughts influenced more than 29 million. India faced severe devastation during the 2024 monsoon season, impacting over 8 million individuals predominantly in the regions of Gujarat, Maharashtra, and Tripura.

From 1995 to 2024, over 832,000 individuals worldwide lost their lives due to 9,700 extreme weather-related incidents, inflicting economic damages amounting to more than $4.5 trillion. In 2024, one of the most catastrophic incidents was Typhoon Trami, which struck the Philippines in October, resulting in over a hundred fatalities and impacting millions of lives. Saint Vincent and the Grenadines in the Caribbean suffered the most severe impact that year, with Grenada in the same area experiencing the second highest level of adversity.

The report categorized India, together with the Philippines, Nicaragua, and Haiti, under the “continuous threats” category, indicating that these nations face persistent and recurrent extreme weather conditions. Nations like Haiti, the Philippines, and India, each ranking among the top ten most vulnerable in the CRI, encounter distinct challenges. They endure such frequent onslaughts of floods, heatwaves, or storms that whole areas scarcely have time to recuperate from one disaster before the next one arrives. Dominica, Myanmar, Honduras, and Libya face an elevated threat due to their exposure to exceptionally severe weather conditions. In 2008, Myanmar was devastated by Cyclone Nargis, marking it as one of the most catastrophic events of the past thirty years, tragically claiming approximately 140,000 lives, constituting over 95% of the nation's fatalities during that timeframe.

The CRI analysis highlights that India is significantly impacted by frequent extreme weather events, which have, over time, influenced both its population and economy. The report points out existing data shortcomings for the Global South, suggesting that the real frequency and consequences of such extreme weather might be more severe than currently understood.

“Implementing effective strategies to address loss and damage is crucial, alongside ensuring sufficient climate finance,” stated David Eckstein, senior advisor on climate finance and investments at Germanwatch. “The CRI 2026 outcomes underscore the necessity for COP30 to identify practical strategies for bridging the global ambition shortfall.” Vera Künzel, senior advisor on climate change adaptation and human rights at Germanwatch, noted that nations facing these challenges will encounter overwhelming obstacles without enhanced long-term assistance, particularly in adapting to the climate crisis. Immediate reduction of global emissions is crucial to prevent an increase in fatalities and global economic catastrophes, and it is imperative to speed up adaptation initiatives.
