data.fused_content:
Bangladesh has secured the 13th position in the Long-Term Climate Risk Index (CRI) facing threats like floods, heatwaves, cyclones, and storms. The CRI was compiled by Germanwatch, a global disaster monitoring organization, through an analysis of data concerning casualties and property damage spanning the past three decades, resulting in more than $4.5 trillion in direct damages when adjusted for inflation. Over the span of three decades, Dominica, Myanmar, and Honduras have been identified as the countries most significantly impacted. India and Myanmar, sharing borders with Bangladesh, both find themselves positioned within the top 10 of the index. Libya (ranked 4th), Haiti (5th), and Grenada (6th) are among the remaining countries in the top 10. The Philippines (ranked 7th), Nicaragua (ranked 8th), and The Bahamas (ranked 10th) also appear within the top 10. Not a single one of the eleven belongs to the affluent industrialized countries of the world. Nevertheless, countries with advanced industrial economies like France (12th), Italy (16th), and the United States (18th) rank within the top 30.

The report indicated that over the past 40 years, the number of deaths caused by cyclones in Bangladesh has decreased by over 100 times, thanks to successful risk prevention strategies and adaptive measures. According to the CRI findings, nations within the Global South face heightened susceptibility to the climate crisis. The CRI was prepared by the global disaster watchdog Germanwatch, and chronicles more than 9,700 incidents of extreme weather spanning from 1995 to 2024.

David Eckstein, a co-author of the report, remarked, "Extreme weather events, such as heat waves and storms, present the most significant risk to human life." Immediate reduction of global emissions is crucial to prevent additional human and economic damages, and adequate climate funding he added. David Eckstein, a contributing author of the report, also stated that the findings of the CRI 2026 unequivocally indicate that COP30 needs to devise successful strategies to bridge the global ambition gap, accompanied by tangible, concrete solutions for loss and damage. A rapid reduction in global emissions is imperative.

In 2024, drought remained the predominant weather phenomenon, while heatwave were the main weather events. In 2024, flood occurrences were the predominant weather events. In 2024, Bangladesh, the Philippines, and India saw the highest numbers of people impacted by extreme weather conditions. In Bangladesh, a particularly intense heatwave struck, with temperatures soaring to 43.8 degrees Celsius, impacting over 33 million individuals. Laura Schäfer, a co-author of the report, noted that storms resulted in the most significant financial losses, whereas floods impacted the largest number of individuals.
====ALIGNMENT====
