Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday and appealed for international assistance as Cyclone Ditwah departed from the island and made its way towards the neighboring nation of India. The severe weather conditions have led to the destruction of over 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 individuals to seek refuge in government-operated temporary shelters. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) announced that an additional 798,000 individuals needed aid after being temporarily uprooted due to the floods. Kodippili affirmed that 153 individuals had lost their lives, stating that among them were 11 residents of an elder care home that was flooded in the north-central district of Kurunegala on Saturday afternoon, police said. The fatalities caused by the floods and mudslides due to Cyclone Ditwah have climbed to 153, with an additional 191 individuals unaccounted for.

Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been deployed to collaborate with civilian workers and volunteers. On Saturday, the military successfully rescued 69 bus passengers, among them a German tourist, who had been marooned in the Anuradhapura district following a 24-hour operation. A passenger, recounting the experience from the hospital, explained how navy sailors assisted them in ascending to the roof of an adjacent house by employing ropes to ensure their safe passage through the floodwaters. "We were very lucky," remarked W. M. Shantha. A helicopter was forced to abandon the first rescue effort. Subsequently, naval boats successfully completed the rescue.

In the central district of Badulla, roads continue to be impassable, isolating numerous villages. "Our village mourns the loss of two residents, one of the districts most severely affected," stated Saman Kumara hailing from Maspanna village in Badulla, which ranks among the most severely affected areas. "There is no food, and we are running out of clean water," he communicated to the News Center website over the phone. Others have found refuge in a temple and a house that remain intact.

Approximately one-third of the nation lacked access to electricity and running water due to the collapse of power lines and the flooding of water purification facilities; internet services also experienced interruptions. Chennai Airport in India grounded 54 flights as the cyclone neared. The Sri Lankan authorities have called for global assistance and encouraged Sri Lankans living overseas to contribute financially to aid the communities in distress. India was the first to respond, dispatching two aircraft filled with relief supplies and deploying a pair of transport helicopters accompanied by a rescue team consisting of 22 members. During a pre-scheduled goodwill visit to Colombo, an Indian warship contributed its provisions to aid the victims.

Although most of the country, including the capital, had seen a reduction in rainfall, the northern region of the island continued to experience showers. The flooding intensified in low-lying zones on Saturday, particularly impacting those residing along the Kelani River's banks. Since 2017, the cyclone stands as Sri Lanka's most lethal natural calamity, when floods and landslides resulted in over 200 fatalities and caused the displacement of hundreds of thousands. In June 2003, the most severe flooding since the beginning of the century resulted in the deaths of 254 people.
