COLOMBO, Dec. 9 (Xinhua) Humanitarian flood relief supplies from the Chinese government reached Colombo on Monday morning and were officially transferred to Sri Lankan authorities. Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Qi Zhenhong, and Sri Lanka's Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunathilake, attended the handover ceremony.

Karunathilake expressed sincere gratitude for China's prompt and generous support, emphasizing that China has consistently offered support to Sri Lanka during challenging periods throughout the years. He noted that amidst the current crisis, substantial aid has been extended by the Chinese government, Chinese businesses, and the Chinese community residing in Sri Lanka. He expressed gratitude to the Chinese government and its citizens for their generous support and reiterated his aspiration for the lasting friendship between Sri Lanka and China.

Qi mentioned that in response to this serious natural disaster, all pertinent Chinese agencies promptly took action to offer assistance to their Sri Lankan counterparts. He observed that both Chinese enterprises and the Chinese diaspora in Sri Lanka have proactively engaged in aiding the local disaster relief initiatives. China remains committed to extending support to Sri Lanka to the best of its abilities, as it has consistently done in the past.

The supplies, consisting of tents, bed sheets, life jackets, and blankets, meet the critical needs of Sri Lanka. Cyclone Ditwah has unleashed devastating weather conditions across the nation, resulting in the tragic loss of over 600 lives and impacting over 2 million residents. The Disaster Management Center of Sri Lanka reports that the harsh weather conditions demolished over 4,000 homes and inflicted partial damage on upwards of 60,000 residences across the country.
