The monsoon period in southern Asia extends from June through September, during which nearly 60% of Bangladesh's populace faces flood threats. This year, extreme rainfall has impacted 45% of India, resulting in approximately 1,500 fatalities. Similarly, Pakistan has witnessed the displacement of two million individuals from their homes, with over 800 losing their lives.

A study published in Nature reveals that the tally of excess deaths — the count of fatalities surpassing the anticipated norm — because of rainfall and flooding is probably a considerable undercount. This work is part of an expanding body of knowledge suggesting that many more people are vulnerable to extreme weather events than are being recorded in official data. Experts suggest that this is a significant factor in why the real figures probably exceed the official records.

Economists Tom Bearpark from Princeton University, Ashwin Rode from the University of Chicago, and Archana Patankar from Green Globe Consulting conducted a study to enhance the precision of mortality evaluations linked to rainfall, specifically centering on Mumbai. Their research revealed that from 2006 to 2015, approximately 2,500 fatalities occurred each year due to the monsoon. Significantly, this mortality estimate far exceeds the official statistics reported for the state of Maharashtra, encompassing Mumbai, from 2006 to 2014. The study further indicates that a significant number of these deaths were among young children and women, with the majority residing in informal settlements. Infants under one year, seniors aged 65 and up, and Black individuals faced the highest vulnerability.

Intense downpours present the greatest threat to the most at-risk populations in Mumbai. Mumbai, besides serving as India's economic center, is home to over a million residents living in informal settlements – hazardous, overcrowded dwellings that lack essential amenities like running water, toilet facilities, and electricity. Deaths that occur indirectly due to rainfall or flooding — such as fatalities from electrocution, a waterborne infectious illness, or falling debris — will not list water as the cause of death.

Rachel Young and Solomon Hsiang from the University of California, Berkeley, calculated that between 1930 and 2015, 7,000 to 11,000 additional deaths each year in the United States were linked to tropical cyclones, starkly contrasting with government statistics citing an average of 24 fatalities directly associated with these events. The unrecorded causes encompass factors like restricted or absent access to healthcare, as well as conditions such as sudden infant death syndrome and cardiovascular diseases.

Precisely documenting both the count and reasons for deaths holds significant importance. It provides families with clarity regarding the passing of their loved ones. Understanding that a significantly larger number of individuals face life-threatening risks from extreme weather phenomena is equally important for those holding influential roles in their nations. Approximately a quarter of the global urban populace resides in informal settlements, necessitating intensified efforts to enhance living conditions, as individuals residing in these homes are the most susceptible to severe weather conditions.

These studies must invigorate the dialogues occurring this week at the COP30 climate summit in Belém, Brazil. Preliminary reports from Brazil indicate that expectations for COP30 are modest, even by the usual benchmarks of COP gatherings.
