Colombo: Sri Lanka announced a state of emergency on Saturday following heavy rains and floods that increased the fatality count to 132, with an additional 176 people reported missing and unaccounted for. Over 15,000 homes have been devastated, forcing 78,000 individuals to seek refuge in government-managed temporary shelters.

In response to the widespread destruction caused by a week of heavy rainfall throughout the island, President Anura Kumara Dissanayake enacted emergency legislation, affording him extensive authority to address the crisis. DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda stated, "We have 132 confirmed fatalities and an additional 176 individuals unaccounted for."

The military successfully evacuated 69 bus passengers, among them a German tourist, on Saturday from the Anuradhapura district, employing both a helicopter and naval boats in the operation. A passenger recounted at a local hospital how navy sailors assisted them in reaching the roof of a neighboring house, employing ropes to navigate through the floodwaters safely. "Fortune was on our side as we stood on the roof, a section gave way," he remarked. Three women ended up in the water, yet they were assisted back up to the roof, Shantha stated. A helicopter had to abort an initial rescue attempt because the rotor’s downdraft posed a risk of dislodging the roof where they were perched on.

The central district of Badulla saw its roads remain impassable, severing access to numerous villages and preventing the delivery of essential relief supplies. "Our village is isolated, with all access routes obstructed by landslides, preventing anyone from entering or exiting. We’re facing a scarcity of food and have limited clean water supplies," remarked Saman Kumara, hailing from Maspanna village in Badulla, among the most severely impacted areas.

Approximately one-third of the nation lacked electricity and access to running water due to the collapse of power lines and the flooding of water purification plants, while internet connectivity remains compromised in numerous regions.

On Saturday, Cyclone Ditwah departed from the island and was advancing northward toward neighboring India. Chennai Airport in India has grounded 54 flights due to the impending cyclone, as meteorologists predict severe downpours and gusty winds for the upcoming 48 hours. New landslides have struck the central region of Kandy, situated 115 kilometers (71 miles) to the east of Colombo, resulting in the main access road being submerged at multiple points.

The government has issued an appeal to the international community for help and urged Sri Lankans living overseas to contribute financially to aid the impacted communities. Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya held meetings with diplomats stationed in Colombo to inform them of the current status and request help from their respective governments.

India was the first to respond, dispatching two aircraft filled with essential relief supplies, while an Indian warship docked in Colombo as part of a pre-scheduled goodwill mission offered its provisions to assist the affected individuals. Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi conveyed his sympathy for the fatalities in Sri Lanka, affirming that New Delhi was prepared to provide additional assistance.

Flooding in low-lying areas worsened on Saturday, for those living along the banks of the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo, prompting authorities to issue evacuation orders. The rain had subsided across much of the nation, including the capital, but the northern part of the island continued to have rainfall as a result of the lingering impact of Cyclone Ditwah.

This cyclone marks the most lethal natural catastrophe in Sri Lanka since 2017, when floods and landslides claimed the lives of over 200 individuals and forced hundreds of thousands to seek refuge elsewhere.
