A severe storm in São Paulo resulted in roughly 1.3 million residents losing power and led to the cancellation of nearly 400 flights. Utility Enel faced criticism from São Paulo's mayor, Ricardo Nunes, who called the company "irresponsible," a sentiment echoed by local leaders and residents in similar disputes reported by Bloomberg in 2023 and 2024. Enel noted that the storm produced winds of up to 100 kilometers (about 62 miles) per hour, which caused trees and power lines to fall. While Enel initially restored electricity to 1.2 million of its 2.2 million clients, 300,000 people experienced outages on Thursday, leaving 1 million still without power. The state's commerce federation estimated business and service providers face 1.54 billion reais (around $290 million) in lost revenue due to the outages.

The continuing vulnerability of the energy grid highlights how extreme weather, intensified by rising temperatures, affects communities, levying a considerable economic burden and challenging utility providers. "Every time something happens, we are left behind without electricity," a frustrated Nunes said. "It is the third time this year alone," psychologist Thais Fernandes told the AP, adding, "Neither the company nor these local authorities are to be trusted."

Fortunately, the disruption did not turn deadly, though extreme weather incidents resulting in fatalities are not uncommon. Utility firms like Enel and governments have opportunities to enhance grid resilience and prepare for extreme weather, including improved weather monitoring using advanced technology. Customers can also proactively respond by investing in solar power systems with battery storage, decreasing grid dependency and reducing reliance on less sustainable energy sources like oil and gas.
