Government officials from both federal and Victorian levels are intensifying their support for families and primary producers impacted by January's bushfires in Victoria, building upon previously established disaster recovery initiatives. This fresh assistance complements the existing Commonwealth–State Disaster Recovery Funding Arrangements and the initial aid provided by the Personal Hardship Assistance Program. Premier Jacinta Allan announced that uninsured homeowners who have lost their main residence will be eligible for grants up to $52,250, during her visit to Natimuk, a community severely impacted. “The devastation wrought by these fires has been beyond measure for countless individuals across Victoria – which is why we are providing additional financial and psychological aid to alleviate some of the challenges faced by our farmers and regional communities,” Allan stated.

Federal Minister for Emergency Management Kristy McBain emphasized the Albanese government's collaborative efforts with Victoria during the recovery phase. McBain stated that the Albanese government supports people of Victoria not only in responding to these fires but also throughout the recovery process. Assistance initiatives concentrate on improving access for farmers and livestock.

Programs of assistance are centered on providing farmers and livestock with necessary access. The “Look Over the Farm Gate” initiative, managed by the National Centre for Farmer Health, is scheduled to commence on Jan. 16, and will provide grants of up to $5,000 for local events and activities specifically in farming areas to support mental health and community recovery. Counselors who were active during earlier drought periods are set to either visit impacted farms or connect with farming families through phone calls. Acting Victorian Minister for Agriculture Steve Dimopoulos stated that the strategy aims to address both safety and welfare concerns, and in collaboration with Agriculture Victoria and the Fire Incident Controller, efforts are focused on simplifying the process for farmers to monitor their animals' well-being.

Amidst substantial losses in agricultural zones, the Victorian government is enhancing mental health and business aid for farmers, notably those whose crops and livestock have been affected. The Rural Financial Counselling Service is set to provide complimentary, confidential wellbeing assistance, coupled with financial and business counselling. Animal welfare continues to be a pressing issue for farmers unable to safely revisit their lands. Property owners may submit requests to Agriculture Victoria, which collaborates with the Fire Incident Controller to determine the timing and method for them to inspect their animals.

To facilitate public donations, the Victorian government is initiating the 2026 Victorian Bushfire Appeal, stating that all contributions will be allocated to communities impacted by the fires, with the state covering administrative expenses. Vicki Ward, the Victorian Minister for Natural Disaster Recovery, stated that the appeal aims to distribute funds based on priorities set by local communities, in collaboration with charities and community organisations.

According to the Insurance Council of Australia (ICA insurers have received 1,386 claims since January 7, spanning home, commercial, motor, and business interruption insurance. Preliminary data indicates that approximately 30% of these property claims account for total losses. Insurers have initiated the processing of these claims and are dispatching personnel to impacted regions as access becomes available. From the Castlemaine Recovery Centre, ICA staff and several insurers are assisting policyholders with lodging claims and addressing queries, with plans for further outreach in additional communities as circumstances permit.

In response to ongoing bushfires and extreme weather, general insurers are activating catastrophe protocols. Suncorp has activated its extreme weather protocol in response to the Victorian bushfires and Ex-Tropical Cyclone Koji. By 6pm AEST on January 11, the company had registered approximately 80 home and motor claims associated with Ex-Tropical Cyclone Koji and roughly 60 claims related to the Victorian bushfires. IAG has established a mobile claims unit in Seymour to assist patrons of its affiliated brands, such as NRMA Insurance, CGU, WFI, Rollin RACQ Insurance, and RACV. IAG's Major Event Response Teams have initiated visits to properties in affected neighborhoods to conduct evaluations and undertake urgent repairs. The site is managing in-person claims, organizing temporary housing, and facilitating emergency financial aid, especially for those experiencing power failures.

Health funds are revising their policies for clients in impacted areas. HCF has rolled out a relief initiative for members affected by the Victorian bushfires and other severe weather occurrences, offering eligible members a premium waiver of up to three months, the ability to pause coverage for up to two years, and participation in the HealthyMinds Check-in program. HCF is also providing qualified members in rural and remote locations with complimentary 15-minute online GP consultations via its collaboration with GP2U, ensuring appointments are accessible every day of the week. For insurers and intermediaries, the current bushfire season is emphasizing disaster response abilities, managing a surge in claims, and working alongside government initiatives and health and rural support services as the recovery and rebuilding efforts continue over the upcoming months.
