data.fused_content:
Shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich due to frigid temperatures and fierce winds, triggering an Extreme Weather Response throughout the area. In Saanich, Broadview United Church at 3703 St. Aidan’s St. has launched an Extreme Weather Response (EWR) shelter, offering 20 overnight beds for those without homes, managed by Our Place Society, with financial backing from the District of Saanich provided by BC Housing. In Victoria, the municipality reports the addition of 30 shelter mats, accessible at 525 Johnson St. beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. The facility accommodates individuals of all genders and has a strict no-pet policy.

The District explains that Extreme Weather Response beds are distinct from the standard nightly shelter accommodations; these EWR beds are specifically deployed when a warning of severe weather conditions arises and spaces become operational solely upon the anticipation of dangerous weather conditions. Overnight temperatures are expected to fall to approximately 1 C, with even chillier conditions anticipated further inland. Severe weather conditions fulfill provincial activation standards, which may encompass freezing temperatures, prolonged rain or snowfall.

Alongside the provisional sleeping arrangements, Saanich has made daytime public warming facilities available. Locals are welcome to keep warm at municipal recreation centers and the Saanich municipal hall during their standard hours of operation. The available facilities are Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. The district acknowledges that hours might change during severe weather conditions because of staffing availability. Residents of Saanich have the option to utilize the shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals. Forecasts indicate winds will increase to 30 kilometres per hour, with gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, before easing overnight.
