BOISE COUNTY, Idaho — A portion of State Highway 21 has reopened after adverse weather delayed rock scaling efforts following a rockslide that occurred earlier this month. The Idaho Transportation Department (ITD) revealed on Tuesday that Highway 21 is now accessible again from milepost 93.7 near Grandjean to milepost 105.5 at Banner Summit. This portion of the road, often referred to as "Avalanche Alley," shuts down multiple times each winter due to the threat of avalanches or actual snow slides obstructing the road. On December 9, a rockslide that occurred overnight caused the closure of State Highway 21 between Sourdough and Banner Summit. On Friday, Dec. 19, ITD declared that the road closure would extend throughout the weekend due to safety concerns arising from the area's inclement weather, featuring strong winds and rain. The road resumed operations on December 12 with fewer lanes and enhanced safety protocols. Drivers are now able to pass through the region freely, ITD stated in a press announcement. Motorists are advised to exercise vigilance in mountainous regions, staying attentive to shifting conditions and being on the lookout for falling rocks, particularly during times of wet weather or fluctuations in temperature.
