Throughout 2025, global temperatures persisted at unusually high levels, even without the warming influence of natural cycles like El Niño, underscoring the significant impact of climate change driven by fossil fuels in exacerbating extreme weather conditions. Heatwaves grew more severe, storms gathered more moisture and increased in strength, while droughts and wildfires deteriorated further, pushing millions of people—especially the poor and marginalized—close to the limits of adaptation.

A recent report disclosed on December 30, 2025, highlighted that global temperatures persisted at unusually high levels for the entirety of the year, and 2025 still ranked among the three hottest years on record, with the three-year global average temperature crossing the 1.5°C threshold for the first time. Since the Paris Agreement came into effect, global temperatures have climbed by approximately 0.3°C. Heatwaves have significantly intensified, with the likelihood of certain events increasing up to tenfold compared to ten years ago.

The report, titled Unequal Evidence and Impacts, Limits to Adaptation: Extreme Weather in 2025, provides a detailed examination of recent climatic phenomena. The report analyzed how the persistent combustion of coal, oil, and gas has intensified severe weather patterns across regions and exacerbated both social and scientific disparities. WWA recorded 157 extreme weather events that satisfied its criteria for humanitarian impact. Floods and heatwaves topped the list with 49 occurrences each, succeeded by storms (38), wildfires (11), droughts (7), and cold spells (3). Heatwaves emerged as the most lethal occurrences of the year; one research report suggested that a solitary summer heatwave resulted in 24,400 deaths across Europe.

Research from 2025 on attribution studies revealed that human-driven climate change has exacerbated heatwaves in countries such as South Sudan, Burkina Faso, Norway, Sweden, Mexico, Argentina, and England. Research into significant wildfires in locations like Los Angeles and southern Spain has revealed that climate change has notably heightened the likelihood of extreme fire weather conditions. Multiple areas, such as central Africa, western Australia, central Brazil, Canada, and extensive sections of the Middle East, encountered some of their driest years on record. All Atlantic hurricanes during the 2025 season intensified as a result of rising ocean temperatures—alterations which could lead to significantly increased destruction.

“Annually, the threats posed by climate change shift from theoretical to starkly tangible,” stated Friederike Otto, a climate science professor at Imperial College London and co-founder of WWA. "The ongoing escalation of greenhouse gas emissions has driven our climate into a heightened state of extremity, where even minor upticks in global temperatures lead to disproportionately severe consequences," she said. Sjoukje Philip, a researcher from the Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute (KNMI), has stated that the extent of warming observed this year cannot be attributed solely to natural climate variability.

Severe weather events invariably impact impoverished and marginalized communities the most, yet shortcomings in weather monitoring and the restrictions of climate models hinder scientists from comprehensively evaluating numerous catastrophes in the Global South. WWA determined that although preparedness and adaptation are crucial, they alone are insufficient. Leaders must confront the fact that their persistent dependence on fossil fuels is resulting in loss of life, substantial financial setbacks, and enduring harm to communities across the globe. Scientists caution that without swiftly moving away from fossil fuels, extreme weather events will grow more severe, challenging societies to manage beyond their limits in the future.
