COLOMBO - Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday, seeking global aid as fatalities from floods and landslides induced by Cyclone Ditwah climbed to 153, with 191 individuals unaccounted for. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that severe weather conditions have devastated more than 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 individuals into government-run temporary shelters, and that 798,000 people needed aid after being temporarily displaced by the floods.

Personnel from the army, navy, and air force, along with civilian workers and volunteers, have been mobilized to support relief operations. In the central district of Badulla, roads remained impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. About a third of the country was without electricity or running water because power lines had collapsed and water purification facilities were inundated. Clean water was scarce, and internet connections were disrupted.

While hospitalized, a passenger recounted how navy sailors assisted them in reaching the roof of a nearby house, using ropes to navigate through the rising floodwaters. A helicopter's initial rescue attempt was thwarted when the rotor's downdraft risked dislodging the roof they were on, but naval boats subsequently completed the rescue.

Cyclone Ditwah departed the island on Saturday, heading towards neighboring India. Chennai Airport in India cancelled 54 flights, with forecasts predicting intense rain and high winds for the following 48 hours.

The Sri Lankan government has called for international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to donate money to support affected communities. An Indian warship, on a pre-scheduled goodwill visit to Colombo, contributed its provisions to assist the victims, as did an Indian warship already in Colombo on a previously planned goodwill visit, which donated its rations.
