Alicante is poised to face severe weather conditions precisely one year after the catastrophic floods that claimed 220 lives during Spain's most devastating natural catastrophe in recent decades. Travelers from the UK planning a visit to the Costa Blanca have been cautioned following an uncommon weather advisory for heavy rain issued for the holiday hotspot. Storm Alice is set to hit the Spanish mainland, which experienced lethal floods the previous year.

A seldom-seen red alert has been declared for the southeastern coast of Spain, escalating the warnings released earlier this week and putting the region on high alert due to the impending "extraordinary danger." AEMET, Spain's meteorological authority, issued a warning on Thursday of potential heavy rainfall in Alicante, a region that witnessed the tragic loss of 220 lives during devastating floods last October. The measure will begin at 10am local time on Friday. The forecaster indicated: "Intense and prolonged rainfall is anticipated through Monday. These showers could lead to localized flash flooding in low-lying regions, streams, and gullies, significantly elevating the risk level to an extraordinary danger."

On Wednesday, significant portions of the Costa Blanca, encompassing well-known tourist spots such as Alicante, Benidorm, and Valencia, were issued yellow alerts. On Friday, AEMET escalated the warning to a red alert for intense rainfall covering the entirety of Alicante and portions of Murcia. Meanwhile, Valencia's advisory has been raised to amber, anticipating up to 140 mm of rain within the forthcoming 12 hours.

The UK Foreign Office has advised British travelers heading to Spain to be cautious, stating: "There are severe weather alerts and potential travel disruptions. Today's alerts affect the regions of Murcia and Valencia, encompassing the province of Alicante." They announced: "Severe weather and travel disruption. Disruptions to travel are likely" and advised travelers to adhere to the guidance from local officials, keep an eye on regional weather reports, and review any communications from their airline or travel provider before embarking on their journey.

Brit Jerome Torres, aged 49, recounted the terrifying experience of listening to his neighbors' last cries as the devastating floods swept through. He revealed that he heard their screams just before they perished. Torres remarked that the Spanish city resembled a scene from a "horror film" having been ravaged by torrential flooding, commencing last Monday. He mentioned he heard them before they died. Torres described the Spanish city as resembling a scene from a "horror film" It suffered severe damage due to relentless flooding, which left homes and businesses in ruins and streets blanketed in a dense coat of mud.

It marked the most devastating flood disaster in Europe since 1967, a year when Portugal saw the tragic loss of at least 500 lives.
