A lightning flash stretching 515 miles from Texas to Missouri has been recognized as the longest electrical bolt in the world. The finding emerged while reviewing satellite imagery of the 2017 storm, surpassing a previous record-holder by approximately 38 miles. Until now, the longest lightning strike was thought to have occurred and reached from —measuring 477 miles long, spanning across Texas, Louisiana, and Mississippi.

The World Meteorological Organization set this new benchmark, noting that each of the lightning occurrences were identified as "megaflashes" due to their nature as "extremely long duration and distance lightning discharge events," and that the enormous lightning flashes of 2017 and 2020 took place within a recognized "storm hotspot" located in the Great Plains area of the United States. The WMO reported that this unprecedented lightning bolt originated within a significant thunderstorm system, and "A car would require approximately eight to nine hours and a commercial aircraft at least 90 minutes to traverse that distance."

According to Professor Randall Cerveny from Weather and Climate Extremes at WMO, progress in scientific and technological fields enables the evaluation of extreme weather phenomena, such as measuring lightning distance. Cerveny remarked, "It is likely that even greater extremes still exist" and that we will be able to observe them as additional high-quality lightning measurements accumulate over time. He also stated, "This new record clearly demonstrates the incredible power of the natural environment."

The NOAA GOES-16 satellite, part of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite program, documented images of the megaflashes in both 2017 and 2020. Introduced in 2016, GOES-16 marked the beginning of the GOES-R satellite series, which comprises four satellites in total, with the last satellite launched in June 2024. GOES-16 remains positioned over specific points on the Earth's surface.

NOAA and NASA collaborate on behalf of the U.S. government to observe and analyze lightning, enhancing weather understanding and forecasting abilities. The United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes each year. Lightning is associated with 24,000 fatalities and 240,000 injuries.

The National Centers for Environmental Information within NOAA monitors the financial impact of extreme weather events, which amounts to billions of dollars. In 2024, they documented 27 significant natural disasters, comprising 17 severe storms such as tornado outbreaks, high winds, and hailstorms, alongside 5 tropical cyclones and 2 winter storm/cold waves. According to NCEI’s website, the total cost from these 27 events in 2024 was $182.7 billion. This follows 2023 in which there were a record 28 separate billion-dollar events. In January, NCEI announced that the financial impact of natural disasters in the U.S. this year could increase by several billion dollars relative to 2024. Wildfires, droughts, and heatwaves are also part of these costly billion-dollar occurrences, and countless wildfires and financial losses amounting to millions in property damage annually. Anticipated rises in severe weather incidents, elevated temperatures, and various shifting climate patterns are likely to lead to more intense storms.
