A recent study has highlighted that Malta’s economy ranks as one of the most susceptible within the European Union to the impacts of extreme weather conditions. Specialists from the European Central Bank and the University of Mannheim project that the occurrence of heatwaves, droughts, and floods across Europe this summer will diminish economic activity by €43 billion in 2025, representing 0.26 per cent of the EU's output. By 2029, it is anticipated that the persistent repercussions of these occurrences will diminish regional economies by €126 billion, in contrast to a scenario devoid of extreme weather conditions.

The research underscores the uneven effects on smaller economies like Malta, Cyprus, and Bulgaria. Malta’s Gross Value Added (GVA), an indicator of economic performance, is anticipated to decline by €236.5 million in 2025, representing 1.14 per cent of its 2024 economic output. Per individual, this equates to approximately €401 in 2025, increasing to €1,031 by 2029. By 2029, the deficit might escalate to €609 million, equivalent to 2.93 per cent.

The report advocates broadening water supply sources and promoting the sustainable utilization of unconventional water resources. According to a recent report from the Energy and Water Agency, Malta faced weather patterns akin to desert climates from September 2023 to August 2024.

Throughout Europe, nations in the south like Spain, Italy, Portugal, and Greece encounter the greatest absolute losses, mainly due to droughts and heatwaves. In contrast, countries in the north and center, such as Germany, Denmark, and Austria, experience more flooding, yet the financial impact is comparatively less severe.

The report highlights, at the EU level, that these assessments are lacking, primarily due to their omission of the cumulative impact of simultaneous droughts and heatwaves in a given area, as well as their failure to incorporate the occurrence of wildfires. The authors caution that the economic repercussions of extreme weather can linger for extended periods. The research indicates that "extreme weather occurrences are progressively exerting significant economic effects across Europe." The most significant impact frequently emerges several years post-event, even in areas where no subsequent extreme events happen. The adverse consequences of extreme weather incidents on European areas intensify. As climate change persists, these repercussions are expected to intensify in the years ahead.
