Calgary’s fierce hailstorms last year resulted in insured damages nearing $2.8 billion, marking it as the second priciest natural disaster in Canadian history, as reported by the Insurance Bureau of Canada (IBC). Both meteorologists and insurers observe a concerning pattern: hail occurrences in Alberta are becoming increasingly frequent, intense, and costly for homeowners and the insurance sector alike. The rise in insurance claims due to hail damage has been significant, with Alberta topping the nation in both claim frequency and expense.

The growing popularity of Class 4 impact-resistant shingles is notable, engineered to endure the impact of large hailstones and severe weather conditions. No Payne Roofing has made these shingles the exclusive choice for their installations, stating they offer long-lasting durability and minimize damage to homes in Calgary. “Class 4 shingles may eventually become a building code standard,” said Mike Payne, owner of No Payne Roofing, “for now, they offer an excellent means to save on time, expenses, and the inconvenience of insurance claims.”

Despite the robust protection these shingles provide, the condition of the underlying structure remains crucial. Plywood generally lasts around 50 years under optimal circumstances, though harsh weather can shorten this lifespan. Payne emphasizes that replacing old plywood during re-roofing is a critical yet frequently neglected task. Many older homes in Calgary feature plywood roof decking, installed over four decades ago. Laying new roofing on aging plywood may cause performance problems and potentially nullify manufacturer warranties. His Calgary team guarantees that the plywood they install will match the lifespan of your new shingles, ensuring both resilient shingles and sturdy underlying structures. This aligns with a broader industry initiative to address Alberta's increasingly harsh weather conditions.
