Intense rainfall throughout the past week has inundated southern and central Taiwan, as the heavy showers induced by the southwest monsoon deliver yet another setback to an area still recuperating from Typhoon Danas last month. Downpours have led to floods, landslides, and road closures, severely damaging properties and prompting the evacuation of thousands. Schools and offices in certain regions were closed throughout the week. Several weather stations reported rainfall surpassing 100mm per hour, notably recording 122mm per hour in Pingtung County’s Sandimen Township. The CWA characterized rainfall exceeding 80mm per hour as “creating a sensation akin to pressure or suffocation, as though the sky is falling; surpassing most urban drainage systems’ capacities; and can readily trigger floods and landslides.”

The storm’s fierce winds and heavy rainfall led to agricultural losses exceeding NT$2.6 billion (US$86.6 million), while also wreaking havoc on over 23,000 roofs and toppling a record-breaking nearly 2,500 utility poles, resulting in widespread power failures.

During last month's turbulent recall campaigns coinciding with the typhoon's impact, several lawmakers from the Chinese Nationalist Party (KMT) exploited the timing to rally support against the recalls. Taichung Mayor Lu Shiow-yen (盧秀燕) of the KMT asserted at anti-recall gatherings that DPP lawmakers were solely preoccupied with the campaigns. KMT Legislator Niu Hsu-ting (牛煦庭) criticized the sharing of photos depicting Taiwan Power Co workers repairing utility poles post-typhoon, labeling it as "emotional blackmail" of voters, especially since KMT lawmakers had previously reduced a subsidy for the utility. However, Chiayi Mayor Huang Min-hui (黃敏惠) from the KMT expressed gratitude towards Kaohsiung Mayor Chen Chi-mai (陳其邁) of the DPP for quickly dispatching personnel to assist Chiayi City with relief efforts. The KMT recently shared an image of Tainan Mayor Huang Wei-che (黃偉哲) from the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) attending a recall event in June, accusing him of prioritizing recall efforts over disaster relief; however, at the time the photo was captured, the typhoon had not yet developed.

The unsuccessful recall attempts on July 26 revealed the efficacy of the KMT's communication strategy, yet the use of biased narratives and misinformation undermine the public's ability to discern information critically. As the KMT legislative caucus persisted last week in their unfounded critiques of the government's flood management initiatives, it is essential for the government to address this issue earnestly and devise clear and swift methods to communicate facts and policies effectively, aiming to reduce the dissemination of damaging misinformation, not only from opposition groups but also from China.

The Central Weather Administration (CWA) shared a chart on Facebook illustrating the intensity of the brief yet intense rainfall that affected southern Taiwan, connecting hourly rainfall statistics with "common perceptions" to aid public comprehension. For instance, it equated less than 0.1mm of rain per hour to “the refreshing mist from water sprayers at summer amusement parks.” The CWA's initiative to inform the public about scientific facts using clear and straightforward language exemplifies impartial public communication. This communication must be woven throughout government channels to restore public confidence and fortify resilience.
