A Senate inquiry has been approved into a widespread algal bloom detected near the Fleurieu Peninsula in March, which has claimed the lives of tens of thousands of marine creatures. The spread along the coastline has led to the introduction of a $28 million investment package, co-financed by the South Australian and federal governments, triggering a collaborative investment scheme aimed at clean-up, recovery, research, and industry assistance. On Tuesday, Premier Peter Malinauskas declared that the SA government would provide a matching $14 million commitment. The package allocates $8.5 million toward a coastal science and research network designed to enhance the early detection and monitoring of harmful algal bloom species. A rapid assessment costing $3 million will evaluate the effects of the bloom on fish stocks and fisheries, while a newly established $2 million national testing lab in SA will eliminate the necessity of sending samples to New Zealand for analysis. Labor, the coalition and the Greens have backed the inquiry. The inquiry provides an opportunity to deeply understand the tangible consequences of this environmental/ecological catastrophe and evaluate the suitability of the existing disaster relief funding framework. Nationals senator Ross Cadell remarked that the widespread backing indicated the matter transcended political boundaries, stating, "Whether it be flood, drought, bushfire, or algal bloom, our communities need support, not politics." He added, "This investigation offers an opportunity to fully understand the tangible consequences of this ecological catastrophe." The algal bloom was described as "unprecedented, and it underscores the urgency of climate action," and a dense layer of foam has appeared on beaches.
