The intensification of climate breakdown has led to droughts and floods, resulting in a shortfall of more than 7 million tonnes in wheat production. Farmers faced unprecedented heatwaves, arid conditions, and restrictions on irrigation, confronting significant challenges in sowing their crops. The outlook for 2025 points to another challenging year for agricultural yields, and winegrowers predict it will be a standout year for vintages, attributing this to the abundance of sunny days.

This decade has witnessed two of the top three poorest harvests in history, and this decade has already seen some of the worst harvests on record after extreme rainfall made it impossible for farmers to drill and manage crops. The climate crisis introduces unpredictability for farmers, causing various growers to face alternating periods of prosperity and decline. This trend is driven by climate change, which is leading to extended stretches of arid conditions interspersed with intense, heavy rainfall capable of causing flooding. The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024.

Millers are bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat to produce flour for bread, cakes, and biscuits. Last autumn, the wheat imports were twice the five-year average. Global wheat shortages are intensifying supply challenges internationally, as various countries face their own issues due to conflicts and adverse weather conditions reducing their crop outputs.

Colin Chappell, an arable farmer from Lincolnshire, said: “I have never known a more challenging time to farm. As a farmer, I am used to managing the worst the weather has to throw at me, but recent years have become impossible. Now, I'm confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest. As a farmer accustomed to dealing with the harshest weather conditions, recent years have presented challenges that are insurmountable. Extended periods of heavy rainfall are succeeded by relentless droughts, with no respite in between, leaving me staring down the barrel of a second consecutive disastrous harvest.”

Tom Lancaster, an analyst specializing in land, food, and farming at the ECIU, commented: “This is the reality of farming amid a shifting climate. Adverse weather conditions are diminishing the British nature of our bread, as millers increasingly rely on imports to compensate for reduced UK production, a trend expected to worsen with the escalation of extreme weather in the years to come. This is what farming in a changing climate looks like. Here’s a glimpse of agriculture under the pressure of climate change, leading to significant financial losses for British farmers, with billions in income evaporating due to increasingly frequent extreme weather conditions escalating in the years to come.”

Chappell added: “I'm seeking additional assistance to manage these challenges and strengthen my business's resilience, yet the closure of green farming initiatives in England means that aid is currently unavailable to me. Reopening these programs must be prioritized to ensure farmers can keep cultivating wheat and other essential crops for the British populace.”
