A coastal storm inundated two Alaska Native villages, Kipnuk and Kwigillingok, in the isolated southwest region of the state on Sunday, forcing more than 1,500 individuals to evacuate and unleashing severe flooding as ocean water surged onto the land, as documented by Yale Climate Connections. One individual has been confirmed dead, while two others are still unaccounted for. Authorities verified that storm effects were observed in 49 villages across the region.

In Kipnuk, the ocean surged 6.6 feet above usual peak tides, surpassing the prior record of 4.7 feet set in November 2000, infiltrating the majority of buildings. The village has endured over 30 instances of flooding since 1979, comprising declared disasters from the years 1979, 1982, 2006, 2009, 2015, and 2022. Kipnuk is located approximately four miles inland from the coast. The village received a $20 million grant from the EPA for construction of protective measures, yet the funding was withdrawn in May.

Researchers concluded that human actions increased the likelihood of the elevated Bering Sea temperatures recorded a day prior to the storm by four to 10 times. Studies indicate that the increasing temperatures associated with human activities have generally intensified storms, even in far northern regions where they usually diminish in strength. Storms moving over warmer ocean waters have the potential to maintain their intensity or even intensify.

Plans to enhance Kipnuk's defenses were scheduled to commence next summer through a program supporting communities dealing with environmental threats, and authorities persist in their quest for additional funding. In September of last year, a state report highlighted the need for enhanced defenses in Kipnuk and proposed strategies for improvement. Groups like the Western Alaska Disaster Relief Fund and Napakiak Disaster Relief are gathering contributions to provide assistance. Reach out to your elected representatives to voice your endorsement for financial backing of community safeguards. If you reside in areas prone to severe weather, clear away nearby plants, secure any outdoor objects indoors, and verify your insurance coverage.
