A rapidly advancing wildfire has destroyed homes in a California Gold Rush town established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. The blaze, part of the TCU September Lightning Complex, has scorched over 19 square miles across various areas in Calaveras and Tuolumne counties and remained uncontained as of early Wednesday.

The fire, dubbed the 6-5 Fire and ignited by lightning, is among over a dozen that sprang up across California on Tuesday, according to CalFire. In the rural town, a minimum of five homes, comprising a blend of standalone and mobile residences, were engulfed in flames. A recreational vehicle also sustained damage.

The blaze prompted the evacuation of Chinese Camp and nearby roads on Tuesday. Seven individuals worked for approximately half an hour outside a residence, swiftly clearing large tree branches and tossing sand onto the flames to prevent the fire from spreading.

Fire officials have called for more assets, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft.

During the Gold Rush, thousands of Chinese immigrants arrived in California and encountered significant persecution, including the imposition of an excessive Foreign Miners Tax aimed at deterring them from engaging in mining activities. Chinese miners established Chinese Camp after being expelled from a neighboring camp; initially named Camp Washington, it was renamed to acknowledge the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers. No instant accounts of casualties or fatalities were available.
====ALIGNMENT====
[
