Recent climate breakdown has increased the likelihood of extreme weather conditions, which triggered “astonishing” fires in Spain and Portugal last month, by a factor of 40, according to preliminary analysis. The lethal wildfires that ravaged 500,000 hectares (1.2 million acres) across the Iberian peninsula in just weeks were 30% more intense than scientists had anticipated in a scenario unaffected by climate change, and 30% more severe than scientists would anticipate in a climate change-free world. Researchers from the World Weather Attribution network determined that these conditions might have arisen once every 500 years in the preindustrial climate, whereas now they are anticipated to occur every 15 years.

The research, based on direct weather observations instead of typical climate model analyses, indicates that prior to industrialization, the likelihood of experiencing 10-day maximum temperatures in the region last month was once projected to occur once every 2,500 years. Now, this phenomenon is anticipated to happen every 13 years. The influence of climate disruption on extreme heat was even more pronounced.

“Climate change is exacerbating hotter, drier, and increasingly flammable conditions, leading to fires that burn with unmatched ferocity,” noted Clair Barnes, a climate scientist at Imperial College London and one of the study's co-authors. Alterations in land utilization have heightened the escalating regional threat of uncontrolled wildfires, as rural youth migrate to urban areas, abandoning farmland and resulting in neglected fields with dense vegetation that is highly flammable. David Garcia, an applied mathematician at the University of Alicante, noted that discussions in Spain have centered on diminishing rural activities and the consequent expansion of vegetation.

“Nonetheless, the significant impact of climate change on these fires remains largely under-discussed, despite clear evidence of its substantial influence,” Sánchez commented. Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez unveiled specifics of a comprehensive 10-point climate initiative designed to address the growing severity of extreme weather conditions on Monday during a discussion with the Guardian, and cautioned that mainstream rightwing parties were jeopardizing climate initiatives by embracing far-right rhetoric. “The issue confronting us today is that conventional right-wing parties might not refute the scientific truth, yet they conduct themselves as though climate change is not a reality.”
