Hurricane Beryl distinguished itself as the inaugural major hurricane of the 2024 Atlantic season, marking the earliest occurrence of a category 5 hurricane on record. As it passed roughly 150 km south of Barbados, it hit the island as a category 3 storm, resulting in large swells and waves impacting the south and west coasts. The storm’s impact primarily manifested as damage, which comprised 58% of the total effects, with subsequent losses at 36% and additional expenses at 5%. The sectors of tourism, fisheries, agriculture, and the environment collectively represented 84% of these impacts. Although it caused only moderate harm to fundamental infrastructure and did not incite a humanitarian emergency, coastal assets suffered substantial damage. The storm's total impact was valued at 193 million Barbados dollars, representing 0.15% of the nation’s GDP. This premature emergence and its repercussions on Barbados underscore the growing unpredictability of climate patterns, heightening the susceptibility of Caribbean small island developing States to severe weather phenomena. Thus, it is crucial to consistently enhance all stages of disaster risk management, encompassing risk assessment, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, and response, empowering nations and communities to effectively confront the challenges posed by extreme weather and climate change.
