PESHAWAR, Pakistan (AP) — Heavy monsoon rains have induced flash floods in northern Pakistan, sweeping away multiple tourist vehicles and leaving at least three people dead and 15 unaccounted for. According to Pakistani rescue officials, the unusually heavy monsoon rains since June 26 have resulted in the deaths of at least 225 individuals and left over 500 others injured nationwide. Specialists assert that climate change is responsible for a rise in extreme weather incidents within the area.

A torrential downpour triggered flooding and landslides on Monday, leaving over 200 local tourists stranded and blocking a major highway. Several vehicles were engulfed by landslide debris, and three bodies have been retrieved. Army helicopters facilitated the evacuation process. Rescuers are employing heavy equipment to locate the missing tourists and residents.

The National Disaster Management Authority released a flood update days ago, advising against travel to northern regions due to the risk of landslides and road obstructions. Officials cautioned that another bout of severe weather, akin to the 2022 floods that inundated one-third of the nation and resulted in 1,737 fatalities, cannot be dismissed.
