North Sumatra has been battered by severe weather for several days, leading to flooding in areas of the Tapanuli Selatan district since Monday, as reported by the national disaster agency BNPB. In Tapanuli Selatan, floods and landslides have tragically claimed eight lives, injured 58 individuals, and forced the evacuation of 2,851 residents, stated Abdul Muhari, a spokesman for BNPB. He noted that flooding and landslides have impacted at least three other districts within the province as well. The local disaster agency is utilizing heavy machinery to remove debris obstructing road access to the district. The Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency attributes the extreme weather to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations with the potential to develop into tropical cyclones. The yearly monsoon, spanning from June to September, frequently results in torrential downpours, causing landslides, flash floods, and the spread of waterborne diseases. The monsoon period, spanning from June to September, is frequently characterized by substantial rainfall. The alteration in climate has influenced storm dynamics, affecting both the length and severity of the season, which results in intensified rainfall, sudden floods, and more powerful wind bursts. This month, landslides caused by intense rainfall in Central Java have resulted in the deaths of at least 38 individuals, with approximately 13 people still unaccounted for.
