On October 28, Hurricane Melissa struck Jamaica, following a period of escalating forecasts and alerts regarding its unprecedented intensity. Hurricane Melissa ranks among the most powerful hurricanes ever to strike the Caribbean, with its origins not being purely natural. Melissa experienced two separate phases of intensification, eventually reaching Category 5 hurricane status upon impact.

Jamaica, as an island nation, faced significant challenges in the wake of Hurricane Melissa, resulting in obstacles to both relief initiatives and damage evaluation processes. Several regional airports were out of service. Fox Weather reported that satellite images of Jamaica, taken after Hurricane Melissa, displayed remarkable alterations to the terrain, showcasing the island's characteristic Caribbean blue waters and vibrant green scenery. Altogether, the comparative "before" images showcased Jamaica's characteristic Caribbean blue waters and vibrant green scenery. Every "before" image was paired with an "after" that depicted the identical location — frequently, the land devoid of foliage, and homes missing from the scene; the water was often murky.

Jamaican Prime Minister Andrew Holness (@andrewholnessjm) posted a segment of his conversation with CNN anchor Anderson Cooper, coupled with a message directed at his constituents. "Your homes might have suffered damage or been completely destroyed, and your communities and towns now appear different. I understand your suffering and share in your grief," Holness wrote.

According to Scientific American's report on Oct. 28, the rise in average temperatures and the warming of ocean waters serve as catalysts for various extreme weather events, including hurricanes, floods, and wildfires. Hurricane Melissa exemplified this phenomenon tragically, though extreme weather events were widespread in 2025. Hurricanes and other severe weather phenomena have long been a part of our environment, yet with the ongoing rise in temperatures, these shifts resemble adding fuel to an already blazing inferno, resulting in orders of magnitude more damage, and is deadlier than before.

Efforts are underway to address the situation. As extreme weather events become increasingly frequent, comprehending and maneuvering through them is essential. People in hurricane-prone areas can stay prepared by keeping a "go bag" ready, following a period marked by escalating forecasts and cautions regarding its history-making severity, gathering essentials such as batteries and water, and always avoiding floodwaters when they surge.
