TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in a California Gold Rush town, originally established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. The area was established circa 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from an adjacent encampment, and endured persecution that included the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry. Chinese Camp emerged as a settlement for Chinese miners who had relocated from a neighboring camp after being expelled, as per Visit Tuolumne County. Initially named Camp Washington, it was later renamed to honor the influx of thousands of Chinese settlers.

The blaze swiftly grew to encompass 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of the town of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. By Wednesday morning, containment efforts had not been successful. The fire is among over a dozen that ignited across California on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire.

At least five residences were ablaze in the rural area, characterized by a combination of standalone and mobile residences. On Tuesday evening, outside a residence, efforts were made in a desperate attempt to keep the blaze from spreading from the neighboring house. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire. A group of seven individuals hastily relocated large tree branches away from the building and shoveled sand onto the flames, striving to prevent the fire from spreading from the house next door. Their efforts lasted approximately 30 minutes until the firefighters arrived.

Fire officials have called for more resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft. Fire authorities have sought extra assistance, including fire trucks, bulldozers, and a plane. CalFire has determined that the 6-5 Fire originated from a lightning strike.

There were no immediate reports of injuries or fatalities.
