Flash floods in Gansu province, northwestern China, have resulted in at least 10 fatalities and 33 individuals reported missing. Beginning on August 7, relentless downpours have led to sudden flooding. By 3:30pm (07:30 GMT) on August 8, the death toll reached 10, with 33 individuals unaccounted for, state broadcaster CCTV reported.

Chinese President Xi Jinping has called for "all-out" rescue efforts, demanding the "utmost effort" to locate and save those who are missing. Xi ordered all regions to “resolutely overcome complacency” and strengthen efforts to identify risks.

Images released by the Gansu government depicted roads blanketed with silt and sizable stones, and video footage posted by the Chinese fire authorities on Weibo depicted rescuers leading villagers through torrents of grey water.

Since late July, torrential rains and resultant floods have claimed the lives of at least 60 individuals in northern China, encompassing regions such as Beijing. In recent weeks, China’s northern and southern regions have been battered by record rainfall, which meteorologists attribute to climate change-induced extreme weather conditions. Flood warnings have been issued in 11 provinces.
