A report has revealed that since 2020, the UK has lost an entire year's supply of bread because extreme weather conditions have devastated crop harvests. Specialists at the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) assess that this quantity of wheat is sufficient to produce over four billion loaves of bread – enough for a year's supply. Climate breakdown has intensified droughts and floods, leading to a shortfall in wheat production exceeding 7 million tonnes.

The intensification of droughts and floods due to climate change has resulted in a shortfall in wheat production. Faced with unprecedented heat, drought conditions, and restrictions on water use, farmers found it challenging to sow their crops. The situation is anticipated to deteriorate further as climate change leads to extended dry spells combined with more intense and severe rainfall, potentially resulting in floods. This decade has seen two of the three poorest harvests ever recorded, with expectations that conditions will deteriorate further.

Throughout this decade, agriculture has faced significant challenges, marked by some of the poorest harvests on record due to excessive rainfall that thwarted farmers' efforts to plant and cultivate their crops. Conversely, this year has presented a different struggle, with crops enduring the harsh conditions of one of the hottest and driest springs and summers documented. The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024.

To produce flour for bread, cakes, and biscuits, millers are bringing in unprecedented quantities of wheat. Last autumn, wheat imports surged to twice the five-year average. The scarcity of wheat is straining international supplies.

Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from Lincolnshire, expressed: "Farming has never been more difficult. As a farmer, although I am accustomed to dealing with the harshest weather, the past few years have been unmanageable. I have never encountered such difficult conditions for farming. Extended periods of rain are succeeded by stretches of drought, appearing relentless, and I am now confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest." He added, “but with the green farming schemes here in England closed, that support isn’t open to me. Getting them open has to be a priority if farmers are to be able to continue to grow wheat and other key crops for the Great British public.”

Tom Lancaster, the analyst specializing in land, food, and farming for the ECIU, commented: "This exemplifies how agriculture must contend with a shifting climate. While we can enhance our efforts to aid farmers in adjusting to these harsh conditions, achieving net-zero emissions is the sole way to avert the worsening of weather-related losses in the future. This exemplifies the reality of farming amidst climate change. Unpredictable weather patterns are rendering our bread less British, compelling millers to rely on imports because of deficiencies in UK yields."
