The Environment Agency has finalized 151 flood management projects throughout England from July last year to 2025. According to ministers, the agency implemented these flood schemes, which included the construction of new defenses and upgrades to existing ones, within the year following Labour's rise to power last July. These initiatives have significantly enhanced flood protection for over 24,000 homes and businesses nationwide.

Completed projects include the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management scheme in Lincolnshire, which aims to better protect 2,904 local properties, and the Cockett Wick seawall improvement scheme in Jaywick, which is better protecting 1,526 properties. In July, work finished on the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme, which renewed three kilometres of tidal defenses in Essex to better protect more than 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

The Government has pledged £2.65 billion over the next two years until April 2026, marking a £250 million increase over the previous allocation, and a total of £7.9 billion in flood defenses from 2026 to 2036, bringing the total to more than £10.5 billion over the 12 years from 2024. Ministers say the investment is set to benefit more than 890,000 properties, protecting businesses, safeguarding farmland and allowing the Government to push ahead with its plans to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029.

The number of UK properties at risk from flooding is growing amid rising sea levels and more volatile rainfall patterns, as well as outdated flood defenses and increased development projects. The Environment Agency says 6.3 million properties in England are currently at risk and estimates that this number will rise to around eight million (one in four properties) by 2050. "Our teams and partners have worked diligently to implement another 151 crucial flood schemes nationwide, ready to protect people and homes this winter," ministers stated. The agency aims to safeguard 52,000 properties by April 2026.
